HUB.add({
  id: 'storybook', title: 'Storybook', short: 'Storybook',
  blurb: 'Stories, args, controls, docs, addons, testing, Chromatic, deployment and design systems.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is Storybook, and why is it used in React applications?`,
  k: `A toy showroom: every brick gets its own shelf where you can play with it in every colour and size.`,
  a: `A workshop for building, documenting and testing UI components in isolation. Each "story" captures a component state. Benefits: faster component development, living documentation, design-system catalogs, interaction, accessibility and visual testing. Current major versions (9/10) bundle testing, controls and docs into the core and use Vite by default.` },
{ q: `How do you set up Storybook in a React application?`,
  k: `One command sets up the showroom.`,
  a: `<code>npx storybook@latest init</code> detects the framework (e.g. <code>@storybook/react-vite</code>), creates <code>.storybook/main</code> and <code>preview</code> files and examples; run <code>npm run storybook</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of stories in Storybook?`,
  k: `A story is one way the toy can look: "big red button", "disabled button".`,
  a: `A story is a function or object rendering a component in a specific state with given args. Stories double as test cases (interaction, visual, a11y), documentation examples, and a reference for designers and QA.` },
{ q: `How do you create a basic story in Storybook?`,
  k: `Write which toy it is and the settings for each shelf.`,
  a: `Use Component Story Format 3 (CSF3): a default export with metadata, named exports as stories.`,
  c: `// Button.stories.tsx
import type { Meta, StoryObj } from '@storybook/react-vite'
import { Button } from './Button'

const meta = { component: Button, args: { children: 'Save' } } satisfies Meta<typeof Button>
export default meta
type Story = StoryObj<typeof meta>

export const Primary: Story = { args: { variant: 'primary' } }
export const Disabled: Story = { args: { disabled: true } }` },
{ q: `How do you use addons in Storybook?`,
  k: `Add extra tools to the showroom, like a ruler or colour checker.`,
  a: `Install the package and list it in <code>.storybook/main</code> <code>addons</code> (or <code>npx storybook add &lt;addon&gt;</code>). Many former addons (controls, actions, viewport, backgrounds, interactions) are built into the core in v9+.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the Knobs addon in Storybook?`,
  k: `Old dials for changing a toy's settings; replaced by better dials.`,
  a: `Knobs let you edit props live via UI knobs defined in the story. Deprecated and removed; replaced by <b>Controls</b>, which are generated automatically from args and component types.`,
  u: `Legacy. Answer with Controls/args.` },
{ q: `How do you handle actions in Storybook?`,
  k: `When you press the toy's button, a log shows that it was pressed.`,
  a: `Pass spy functions as args (<code>fn()</code> from <code>storybook/test</code>) or configure <code>argTypesRegex</code>; calls appear in the Actions panel and can be asserted in play functions.`,
  c: `import { fn } from 'storybook/test'
const meta = { component: Button, args: { onClick: fn() } }` },
{ q: `How do you use the Controls addon in Storybook?`,
  k: `Dials that appear automatically for each setting.`,
  a: `Controls are generated from <code>args</code> and inferred prop types (TypeScript/docgen). Customise with <code>argTypes</code> (control type, options, description), and hide or disable as needed.`,
  c: `argTypes: { variant: { control: 'radio', options: ['primary', 'ghost'] }, size: { control: { type: 'range', min: 12, max: 24 } } }` },
{ q: `How do you handle component documentation in Storybook?`,
  k: `Each toy shelf comes with an instruction card.`,
  a: `Autodocs generates a docs page from stories, prop types and JSDoc (enable with <code>tags: ['autodocs']</code>). Write MDX docs for guidelines, mixing <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Controls&gt;</code> and prose.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the Docs addon in Storybook?`,
  k: `It turns your shelves into a pretty instruction booklet.`,
  a: `Docs renders documentation pages: autodocs, MDX files, doc blocks (Canvas, ArgTypes, Source, Description). Part of the default setup.` },
{ q: `How do you handle themes in Storybook?`,
  k: `See every toy in light and dark rooms.`,
  a: `Add decorators wrapping stories in your theme provider, and a toolbar global to switch themes (e.g. <code>@storybook/addon-themes</code> with <code>withThemeByClassName</code> or provider-based decorators). Theme Storybook's own UI with <code>storybook/theming</code>.` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with TypeScript?`,
  k: `Label the shelves so settings are checked.`,
  a: `Supported out of the box. Type stories with <code>Meta</code>/<code>StoryObj</code> and <code>satisfies</code> for full arg inference; react-docgen(-typescript) extracts prop types for controls and docs.` },
{ q: `How do you integrate Storybook with existing component libraries?`,
  k: `Bring the toys you already have into the showroom.`,
  a: `Write stories for wrapper components, add global decorators for providers (theme, router, query client), import global CSS in <code>preview</code>, and configure aliases and assets in <code>main</code>.` },
{ q: `What are some best practices for organizing stories in Storybook?`,
  k: `Arrange shelves by room and name them clearly.`,
  a: `Colocate <code>*.stories.tsx</code> next to components; use <code>title</code> hierarchy (Design System/Inputs/Button) or auto titles; one story per meaningful state; sensible default args in meta; reuse args across stories; tags to filter; keep stories pure with mocked data (MSW addon for network).` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with CSS-in-JS libraries?`,
  k: `Bring the paint helper into the showroom.`,
  a: `Add a decorator wrapping stories with the library's provider (styled-components <code>ThemeProvider</code>, Emotion <code>ThemeProvider</code> + cache) and global styles, or use the themes addon's provider helper.`,
  c: `export const decorators = [Story => <ThemeProvider theme={theme}><GlobalStyle /><Story /></ThemeProvider>]` },
{ q: `How do you handle accessibility testing in Storybook?`,
  k: `Check each toy can be used by everyone.`,
  a: `Use the a11y addon (axe-core) to show violations per story, run them as part of Storybook tests in CI (Vitest addon), and test keyboard flows in play functions.` },
{ q: `How do you use the A11y addon in Storybook?`,
  k: `A checker that lists accessibility problems on each shelf.`,
  a: `Install <code>@storybook/addon-a11y</code>; the Accessibility panel shows axe results with highlights. Configure rules via <code>parameters.a11y</code>, and set <code>a11y.test: 'error'</code> to fail tests on violations.` },
{ q: `How do you handle performance testing in Storybook?`,
  k: `Time how fast each toy moves.`,
  a: `Not a perf tool by itself: use the React DevTools Profiler on stories, community performance addons, Lighthouse on built Storybook pages, and keep stories light. Real performance testing belongs to the app (Web Vitals, Playwright traces).` },
{ q: `How do you use the Storysource addon in Storybook?`,
  k: `Shows the code behind each toy.`,
  a: `Storysource displayed a story's source code in a panel. Deprecated; the Docs <code>&lt;Source&gt;</code> block and "Show code" in docs replace it.`,
  u: `Legacy addon.` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with Jest for snapshot testing?`,
  k: `Take a photo of every shelf automatically.`,
  a: `Reuse stories in tests with <code>composeStories</code> from <code>@storybook/react</code>, render them with RTL and snapshot or assert. The old Storyshots addon is deprecated; modern approach: Storybook's Vitest-based test addon (stories as tests) and visual testing with Chromatic.`,
  c: `const { Primary } = composeStories(stories)
test('primary', () => { render(<Primary />); expect(screen.getByRole('button')).toHaveTextContent('Save') })` },
{ q: `How do you deploy Storybook to a static site?`,
  k: `Print the showroom as a website anyone can visit.`,
  a: `<code>npm run build-storybook</code> outputs <code>storybook-static</code>; host on GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, S3, or Chromatic (which also versions it per branch).` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with CI/CD pipelines?`,
  k: `Every time code changes, robots check the showroom.`,
  a: `In CI: build Storybook, run story tests (interaction + a11y via the Vitest addon or test-runner), run visual tests (Chromatic), and publish previews per PR. Fail the pipeline on errors.` },
{ q: `How do you handle visual regression testing in Storybook?`,
  k: `Take photos of every shelf and spot changes.`,
  a: `Chromatic (by the Storybook team) snapshots each story across browsers and viewports and asks for review on diffs; alternatives: Percy, Loki, Playwright screenshots. Stabilise with fixed data, fonts and disabled animations.` },
{ q: `How do you use the Chromatic tool with Storybook?`,
  k: `A cloud photographer for your showroom.`,
  a: `Create a project, run <code>npx chromatic --project-token=&lt;token&gt;</code> (or the GitHub Action). It uploads Storybook, captures snapshots, diffs against baselines, provides UI review and published Storybook links in PRs, with TurboSnap to test only changed stories.` },
{ q: `How do you debug issues in Storybook?`,
  k: `Look at the showroom's notes when a toy won't show up.`,
  a: `Check the browser console and terminal, run with <code>--debug-webpack</code>/Vite logs, verify <code>main</code> config (framework, stories globs, aliases), missing providers (add decorators), and use <code>npx storybook doctor</code> for version mismatches.` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with different frameworks (e.g., Vue, Angular)?`,
  k: `The showroom works for toys from other toy companies too.`,
  a: `Storybook has framework packages (<code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code>, <code>@storybook/angular</code>, svelte, web-components). Stories use the same CSF format; <b>composition</b> lets one Storybook embed others from different stacks.` },
{ q: `How do you handle internationalization in Storybook?`,
  k: `See every toy's label in different languages.`,
  a: `Add a <code>locale</code> global with a toolbar item and a decorator that wraps stories in your i18n provider (react-i18next, react-intl) and sets <code>dir</code> for RTL languages.` },
{ q: `How do you use Storybook with design systems?`,
  k: `The showroom becomes the official catalogue of all house bricks.`,
  a: `Storybook is the design system's living catalogue: token docs (colors, typography) in MDX, component stories with all variants, guidelines, a11y and visual testing, Figma links (Designs addon), and published versions for consumers.` },
{ q: `How do you optimize Storybook for better performance?`,
  k: `Make the showroom open faster.`,
  a: `Use the Vite builder, lazy compilation, narrower <code>stories</code> globs, fewer heavy addons, the docgen setting that fits (react-docgen is faster than react-docgen-typescript), and TurboSnap in Chromatic.` },
{ q: `How do you handle versioning and release management with Storybook?`,
  k: `Keep an album for each version of the toys.`,
  a: `Publish Storybook per release (or per PR) with Chromatic or versioned folders, align with the component library's semver and changelog (Changesets), and document migration notes in MDX.` },
  ],
});
